<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Clive</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1725">1725</a>–<a href="/years/1774">1774</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Clive of India</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Clive was an officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> whose victories in the Carnatic wars checked French power in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. His victory at Plassey in 1757 over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a>, the nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, made the Company the real power in Bengal, and in 1765 he obtained the diwani of Bengal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-clive.jpg" alt="Robert Clive" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nathaniel Dance, (later Sir Nathaniel Dance-Holland, Bt) (died 1811) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Clive,_1st_Baron_Clive_by_Nathaniel_Dance,_(later_Sir_Nathaniel_Dance-Holland,_Bt).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Clive was born at Styche, the family estate near Market Drayton in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>, the eldest of thirteen children of Richard Clive, a lawyer and member of Parliament. His father obtained for him a post as a writer, or clerk, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, and in 1743 he sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. After the French took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> in 1746, during the First Carnatic War, Clive joined the Company's army. He served in a failed expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Tanjore</a> and nearly lost his small force at Devikottai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The death of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a> in 1748 began the Second Carnatic War, in which the French governor Dupleix used small disciplined forces to tip the balance among Indian rulers. He returned in 1755 as deputy governor of Fort St. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>. In 1756 the new Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, and prisoners were put in the cell known as the Black Hole, where 43 of 64 were reported to have died. Clive retook the city and besieged the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandannagar">Chandannagar</a>. He agreed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a>, who led a conspiracy against the nawab, to make him viceroy in return for payments to the Company. Clive's victory at Plassey in 1757 followed.</p><p>He went home in 1760 with a fortune, entered Parliament and was made an Irish baron. In 1765 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and on 12 August of that year obtained from the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II the diwani of Bengal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>, which made the Company the real ruler of some thirty million people. He raised civil servants' pay and forbade gifts from Indians, though corruption continued. In February 1767 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> for the last time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> he bought Claremont in Surrey, where Capability Brown remodelled the house and garden. Parliamentary debate on the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> famine, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a> and others blamed on Company officials, reduced his political popularity. On 22 November 1774 Clive died at his house in Berkeley Square after cutting his throat with a paper knife; no inquest was held, and newspapers reported an apoplectic fit.</p><p>Clive remains one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s most controversial colonial figures. Lord Macaulay admired him, but modern historians criticise his management of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>'s revenue, which raised demands on the rural population and increased poverty before the famine of 1770. His statue stands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a> and in King Charles Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Clive” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Robert Clive</b>'s victory at the Battle of Plassey in 1757 deposed the Nawab of Bengal Siraj-ud-Daulah in favour of Mir Jafar, and the Battle of Buxar in 1764 forced the emperor Shah Alam II to appoint the Company diwan, or revenue collector, of Bengal, Bihar and Orissa in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seven Years’ War (1756–1763) brought <b>Robert Clive</b>’s victory at the Battle of Plassey in 1757, the conquest of Quebec after James Wolfe’s victory on the Plains of Abraham in 1759 and naval victories at Lagos and Quiberon Bay.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Joseph-Francois Dupleix, governor from 1742, intervened in the succession disputes of the Carnatic Sultanate and Hyderabad, raising Indian troops and briefly dominating the Deccan, until <b>Robert Clive's</b> victories reversed French fortunes.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-india">French India</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Robert Clive's</b> victory at Plassey in 1757, helped by Mir Jafar's betrayal, made the nawabs dependent on the East India Company.</blockquote><a href="/state/nawabate-of-bengal">Nawabate of Bengal</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1756" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Robert Clive"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1756">What was here in 1756? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
